        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "The majority of the $123 billion was directed toward supporting poor children.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "Congressman Scott played a role in the development of the American Rescue Plan.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "The speaker believes that being a former labor union lobbyist gives them credibility when discussing policy outcomes related to labor and education.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker assumes that the success of educational investments should be evaluated based on their impact on disadvantaged students and learning recovery.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker trusts that the figures cited (e.g., $123 billion, 20%) are accurate representations of the plan’s allocation.",
        "confidence": 0.88
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker views the American Rescue Plan as a significant and meaningful intervention in public education.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker expects Congresswoman Scott to have direct knowledge or accountability regarding the implementation and outcomes of the funding.",
        "confidence": 0.85
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 97116,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/7/97116.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 15,
    "turn_text": "We think so, and it's still in the process of being successful. I think we need to take credit where credit is due. This is the largest investment in K-12 education in the history of the United States, and we use the Title I formula as you pointed out, where poverty is the biggest factor in that formula, so the money was most needed. But just a few weeks ago, we had achievement test scores released that showed that students all over the country and in Virginia had not done very well on those achievement tests. That was not a surprise. They were not in school for the better part of two years. So of course they're going to have problems with test scores. The question didn't whether or not they did poorly on the test. The question is what are you going to do about it? And we saw that coming. and put the money there so that when they were able to reopen, they would be able to make up for loss learning. A lot of the school systems are making investments and extra tutorials and extra mentoring and enhance summer programs to make sure that they can catch up. If they don't catch up quickly, particularly the juniors and seniors in high school, if they graduate behind, they will be permanently inflicted with less learning for the rest of their life. We need to make sure they catch up and catch up quickly. And that's what that investment did. We saw the problem coming and the money is certainly there. If you look around Hampton Roads, you see school districts doing a lot of innovative things to make sure the students can catch up.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
