    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 97116,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/7/97116.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 17,
    "turn_text": "Well, it means that the school system has the resources necessary to catch up. I mean, one of the first things, even before the pandemic, a study came out that showed that there was significant problems in ventilation systems all over the country. You can't open a school in an airborne pan during an airborne pandemic if you haven't fixed the ventilation system. That was one of the first investments that had to be made. One of the things to operate safely, you have to have more bus drivers, more buses, because you can't load as many children on a bus during a pandemic. You have to have school nurses, you can't open, you have to school nurses, a lot of schools didn't have nurses. You have to have school counselors, they're coming out of isolation, coming to school with a lot of different issues, PPE, wasn't part of the budget before the pandemic, you need that as part of the budget. There are a lot of things in the budget, and you also need to make up for loss learning. Teachers, assistants, after school programs, and I know Hampton was having makeup sessions, not only on Saturday, they're having makeup sessions on Sunday to make sure that students are catching up. And all of the school systems in the area were having enhanced summer programs. So it's working. Regrettably the test scores that came out were not good but that was expected because schools make a difference and if you're not in school you're going to see that in the test scores and those resources will be going to making sure that the students are catching up.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
